task: dataflow-rescale
agent: opus-dataflow
started: 2026-09-12
branch: feature/dataflow-rescale
worktree: ../okay-wt-rescale
spec: specs/dataflow.md, stage 13

why: the last unbuilt box of the dataflow arc, and the one the
  journal + 6b replay were built to make possible. Partitions are
  fixed at submission today; stage 13 changes the partition count
  between two epochs — stop at N with parts, re-cut the source,
  resume at N+1 with parts'. Verifiable on one machine, which is why
  it is not BLOCKED like stage 12.

what: box 1 first — a stream stops at epoch N and resumes at N+1 with
  a different partition count, and the answer equals the batch answer.
  The mechanism is already here: stopping at N is a coordinator death
  after a commit (Checkpoint saves, then throws), resuming is the
  ordinary journal resume, and a windowed sink REPLAYS from zero on
  resume (stage 11 box 2) — so a re-cut source is read afresh by the
  new partitions and the coordinator's folded state, which is NOT
  per-partition, carries across unchanged. The resume path must seed
  its per-partition vectors (seen, positions) at the NEW count rather
  than the journal's old count; that is the whole of the change.

  A SEEKABLE sink cannot rescale and must say so: its positions are
  per-old-partition offsets that mean nothing under a re-cut. That
  refusal is the same split stage 11 box 2 already found (windowed
  replays, seekable hands over deltas), and it gets a test.

  box 3 (add/remove a worker mid-stream) falls out of the same
  change: the workers vector is a resume argument, so a longer or
  shorter one on resume re-maps partitions to workers — the recovery
  road, without a death.

what this is NOT: box 2 (re-bucketing the boundary panes of a LATE
  feed rather than re-reading them). A re-cut changes the watermark
  trajectory, so a late feed's DROP set can move across a rescale;
  box 1 is asserted on an IN-ORDER feed where nothing drops and the
  answer is cut-invariant. The late-feed re-bucketing is box 2 and is
  left as a stated finding, not faked.

predict: box 1 and box 3 land in one gate; the seekable refusal is
  one throw and one assertion. The risk is the coordinator's ABSORBED
  state (panes retired below the watermark during epochs 1..N) not
  lining up with the new cut's replay — mitigated by the in-order
  contiguous cut (Flow.slices), where a retired pane's elements were
  all consumed by the partition that owned that prefix.
